            "source": "MIM curation (#320/#319)",
            "notes": "identifier and ontology_id CHEBI:28911 -> CHEBI:30411. ChEBI's live definition of CHEBI:28911 is 'A cobalamin in which the central cobalt atom has an oxidation state of +3' \u2014 it specifies an oxidation state this record's label does not. 'Cobalamine' names the vitamer generically, so MAPPING_SEMANTICS \u00a73 step 4 gives it the generic term CHEBI:30411 ('cobalamin'), which was unheld. The record's own CAS 18534-66-2 is registered by ChEBI against cob(I)alamin \u2014 a *third* oxidation state \u2014 which is the #320 conflict that surfaced this and is itself evidence the record was never specific to +3. Definition read from the live EBI OLS4 API, since #319 records that the local ChEBI build lacks definitions for some of these terms."
          },
          {
            "evidence_type": "DATABASE_MATCH",
            "source": "MIM curation (#138/#320)",
            "notes": "Cleared cas_rn, molecular_formula, inchi and smiles, and moved kg_microbe_node_id CHEBI:28911 -> CHEBI:30411. Every one of these encoded the +3 oxidation state this record does not assert. The formula/InChI/SMILES were written by AUTO_BACKFILL_CHEBI_CHEMISTRY on 2026-05-01 by copying CHEBI:28911 while the record was mapped there, so they were a transcription of the grounding rather than evidence for it \u2014 the InChI still ended '/q;+3/p-2'. The cas_rn 18534-66-2 is cob(I)alamin (KEGG C00853, ChEBI:15982, Gmelin 887721), a THIRD oxidation state, and came from a PubChem name lookup \u2014 the mechanism that also put CHES on Beef extract. CHEBI:30411 deliberately carries no formula, charge or mass because its oxidation state is unspecified, so a record grounded there must not carry structure fields that fix one. Leaving them would re-seed the same wrong inference on any future automated re-grading. Note the formula does not discriminate oxidation state in any case: ChEBI records the identical C62H88CoN13O14P for cob(II)alamin and cob(III)alamin, which differ only in the charge field."
          }
